<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joshua Nkomo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1999">1999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joshua Nkomo was a Zimbabwean nationalist who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-peoples-union">Zimbabwe African People's Union</a> (ZAPU) in 1961 and led it through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war against white minority rule in Rhodesia. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-mugabe">Robert Mugabe</a> he formed the Patriotic Front in 1976. After independence he was driven from government in 1982 amid the Gukurahundi repression in Matabeleland, but ZAPU merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanu-pf">ZANU-PF</a> in 1987 and he became vice-president in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joshua-nkomo.jpg" alt="Joshua Nkomo" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob Croes for Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joshua_Nkomo_(1978).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joshua Mqabuko Nyongolo Nkomo was born on 7 June 1917 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matopos-hills">Matopos</a> in Matabeleland, Southern Rhodesia, into a poor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> family, one of eight children. His father, Thomas Nyongolo Letswansto Nkomo, was a preacher and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> rancher who worked for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a>. He trained as a carpenter and taught carpentry at Manyame School in Kezi. In 1942 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, where he attended Adams College and the Jan H. Hofmeyr School of Social Work, which awarded him a diploma in 1952.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulawayo">Bulawayo</a> in 1947 he became a trade unionist for black railway workers, rose to lead the Railway Workers Union and then headed the Southern Rhodesian branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a>. He became chairman of its successor body in 1957, and the National Democratic Party, founded on 1 January 1960 after that body was banned, made him president. When it too was banned in December 1961 he formed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-peoples-union">ZAPU</a> with Ndabaningi Sithole, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-mugabe">Robert Mugabe</a> and others; it was banned in September 1962.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-peoples-union">ZAPU</a> split in 1963, when Sithole and Mugabe took their supporters to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-national-union">Zimbabwe African National Union</a> (ZANU). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ian-smith">Ian Smith</a>'s government held Nkomo at the Gonakudzingwa Restriction Camp from 1964 to 1974, when many detainees were released under pressure from South African Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vorster">John Vorster</a>. He then went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> to pursue both armed resistance and negotiation. ZAPU's armed wing shot down two Air Rhodesia airliners, on 3 September 1978 and 12 February 1979, killing 107 people in all.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancaster-house">Lancaster House</a> talks of September 1979, chaired by Lord Carrington, Nkomo and Mugabe shared a delegation called the Patriotic Front. In the 1980 elections <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-peoples-union">ZAPU</a> won the twenty seats of Matabeleland; Nkomo declined the ceremonial presidency and became Minister of Home Affairs. In 1982 he was accused of plotting a coup after arms were found on ZAPU farms. The Fifth Brigade's Operation Gukurahundi killed up to 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> civilians, and Nkomo fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 7 March 1983.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-peoples-union">ZAPU</a> merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanu-pf">ZANU-PF</a> in 1987, some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> accused him of selling out; he said he had acted to stop the killing of Ndebele and ZAPU organisers. He was sworn in as vice-president on 6 August 1990, a post that was in practice almost powerless. He died of prostate cancer on 1 July 1999 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harare">Harare</a>, aged 82, was declared a National Hero and is buried in the National Heroes' Acre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joshua Nkomo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Patriotic Front of Robert Mugabe and <b>Joshua Nkomo</b>, excluded from the elections, kept fighting, and no country recognised the new state.</blockquote><a href="/state/zimbabwe-rhodesia">Zimbabwe Rhodesia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Semokwe, in what is now <a href="/countries/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harare">Harare</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-20.8,27.82&amp;year=1970" aria-label="Semokwe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5299 2933 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joshua Nkomo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5749 3203h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5749 3203h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-20.8,27.82&amp;year=1970">What was here in 1970? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
